data.content:
Here are the improved MSTest assertions for your collection tests:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetActiveUsers_ReturnsOnlyActiveUsers()
{
    var service = new UserService();
    var users = service.GetActiveUsers();

    Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count);
    Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive));  // no built-in "all match" assertion
    var admin = users.Single(u => u.Role == "Admin");
    Assert.IsNotNull(admin);
}
```

**Key replacements:**

| Instead of | Use |
|---|---|
| `Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0)` | `Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0)` or `Assert.AreNotEqual(0, users.Count)` — MSTest lacks `IsNotEmpty` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(users.Count() == 3)` | `Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count)` — gives actual vs expected on failure |
| `Assert.IsTrue(admin != null)` | `Assert.IsNotNull(admin)` — clearer failure message |

**MSTest's `CollectionAssert`** class is also available for typed collections:

```csharp
CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreNotNull(users.ToList());
CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreUnique(users.ToList());
CollectionAssert.Contains(users.ToList(), expectedUser);
CollectionAssert.AreEqual(expectedList, actualList);      // order matters
CollectionAssert.AreEquivalent(expectedList, actualList); // order ignored
```

> **Note:** `CollectionAssert` methods require `ICollection`, so you may need `.ToList()`.

The main benefit: when tests fail, specific assertions like `AreEqual(3, users.Count)` report *"Expected: 3, Actual: 5"* instead of just *"Assert.IsTrue failed."*
